Engaging in Bodhisattva Behavior|(Text) Author: Shantideva (zhi ba lha). Question: This Shantideva's text was published as "Entering into the Bodhisattva's Way". Does the word "engaging" have a meaning "entering" or does it have some other meaning? Which variant should we use? ALEX: ENGAGING AND ENTERING ARE THE SAME WORD IN TIBETAN = 'JUG-PA. IT IS THE TERM USED IN ENGAGED BODHICHITTA, AS OPPOSED TO ASPIRING BODHICHITTA. IT MEANS TO ENETR INTO THE PRACTICE, WHICH MEANS TO ENGAGE IN THE PRACTICE, TO ACTUALLY DO IT. TRANSLATE MY TEXT TITLE EXACTLY AS IT IS : ENGAGING IN BODHISATTVA BEHAVIOR (NOT BODHISATTVA WAY). IF THERE IS A MORE FAMILIAR RUSSIAN TRANSLATION OF THE TITLE, PUT THAT IN PARENTHESES AFTER MY VERSION Question: Can we translate it as "Practicing the Bodhisattva Behavior" or will it be not the same? ALEX: NOT "PRACTICING" 'JUG-PA (SKT. AVATARA) MEANS TO ENTER INTO SOMETHING, TO ENGAGE IN DOING SOMETHING. Question: We have the problem here, because in Russian it is impossible to say “engaging behavior”. That’s the reason why it was translated as “Engaging in Bodhisattva Way”. It is possible to say “Engaging in practice of Bodhisattva Behavior”. ALEX: CAN YOU SAY ENGAGING IN BODHISATTVA CONDUCT, OR ENGAGING IN THE CONDUCT OF A BODHISATTVA. THAT WOULD BE BETTER. Question: Unfortunately, there is one Russian word both for “behavior” and for “conduct”. So, there is the same problem. ALEX: HOW ABOUT ENGAGING IN THE ACTIONS OF A BODHISATTVA. Question: We also don’t say “engaging in the actions”. Can’t we say “Engaging the Bodhisattva Path” or “Engaging in the Practice of Bodhisattva Behavior”? ALEX: ENGAGING IN THE PRACTICE OF BODHISATTVA BEHAVIOR IS OK. WE NEED THE WORD "BEHAVIOR" IN THE TITLE, SINCE IT IS THERE IN TIBETAN (SPYOD) AND SANSKRIT (CHARYA) Gender of "Bodhicharyavatara": neuter. Skt: Full: Bodhisattvacaryavatara Short: Bodhicarya-avatara.
