Sutra on the Array Like a Woven Basket|(Text) Question: Our Russian word for “array” also means “construction”, so it would be possible to think that this sutra says how to build up the Pure Land of Bliss. Could we use the Russian word that means “arrangement”, “constitution”? ALEX: THE WORD FOR ARRANGEMENT WOULD BE BEST Question: This Russian word has many meanings, including “arrangement”, “constitution” and also “device”. When we translate “The Sutra on the Array of the Pure Land of Bliss”, it is easy to understand that we mean “arrangement”, ALES: SO USE THAT RUSSIAN WORD HERE. Question: but when we translate “The Sutra on the Array Like a Woven Basket” using this word, it seems like “The Sutra on the device <that is> like woven basket”. ALEX: DON'T USE THAT WORD HERE. Question: Is it possible to use the word that means “structure” – “The Sutra on the Structure <that is> Like a Woven Basket”. ALEX: NOT SO GOOD Question: We have one more word for “arrangement”, which means arrangement/disposition/layout/order, and it seems to be quite suitable for “The Sutra on the Array Like a Woven Basket” (“The Sutra on the Disposition Like a Woven Basket”), ALEX: USE THAT WORD HERE Question: but if we use this word for “The Sutra on the Array of the Pure Land of Bliss”, it will be more like “The Sutra on the Location of the Pure Land of Bliss” – the meaning will be totally different. ALEX: DON;T USE THAT WORD HERE. SO, YOU WILL BE USING TWO DIFFERENT WORDS FOR ARRAY. THAT'S OK. Skt: Full: Short: Karandavyuha Sutra.
